  "title": "dynamic programming states, recurrences, and tabulation",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Dynamic programming (DP) avoids repeating work by defining a finite set of subproblems and storing results when the same state is reached through multiple paths.",
    "A state is a compact description of a subproblem; a recurrence specifies how its answer depends on smaller states, and base cases define the smallest states directly.",
    "Top-down memoization follows recursive dependencies and caches results, while bottom-up tabulation computes states in an order that guarantees dependencies are ready.",
    "The number of states multiplied by work per state determines time complexity; state dimensions and stored history determine memory complexity.",
    "State design must retain enough information to make future choices independent of discarded history; missing a dimension can merge subproblems that have different answers.",
    "A dependency graph clarifies valid evaluation order; a DP can often be viewed as shortest or longest paths through a directed acyclic state graph.",
    "Space optimization is valid only when overwritten states are no longer needed; loop direction can change a 0/1 choice into accidental repeated use.",
    "Reconstructing a solution requires preserving choices or recomputing predecessor decisions, while counting, minimizing, and maximizing use different recurrence operators."
  ],
